body_text:
### Proposal Details

This proposal describes adding a default-on, offline server certificate revocation checking mechanism to `crypto/tls` using a local cache conforming to the C2SP [`upki-revocation` specification][c2sp]. After normal certificate verification succeeds, clients consult an available cache and reject leaf certificates reported as revoked. This can only restrict the set of accepted connections, not allow anything that would have been rejected otherwise.

## Background

On Linux, the BSDs, and other platforms without native verifier integration, Go loads system roots but verifies certificates itself, without general revocation checking. Go's [verifier-selection logic][go-verify] uses native verifiers only on Windows, macOS, and iOS with system roots. Pools created with [`x509.NewCertPool`][go-new-cert-pool] also use the pure-Go verifier. Even in cases where native verification is used, revocation checking isn't assured. E.g. Go's [Windows verifier][go-windows] doesn't request CRL or OCSP checks.

[UPKI][upki] provides local revocation checking based on Mozilla's CRLite work, as used in Firefox. It supports independent mirrors of Mozilla's compact aggregated CRL information and avoids both per-connection OCSP availability and privacy concerns and the bandwidth/storage costs of individual CRLs. A shared cache lets administrators update revocation data independently of application releases. Under this proposal, applications built with a supporting Go release benefit without code changes.

UPKI has been [supported by Canonical][canonical], who are already [shipping `upki` as part of Ubuntu 26.10+](https://ubuntu.com/blog/ubuntu-26-10-security). Other distros have done similar (e.g. [NixOS](https://github.com/NixOS/nixpkgs/blob/02c087f3fe673c9e317f206a627cb6b910336dbe/nixos/modules/programs/upki.nix)), or expressed interest (e.g. [Redhat](https://github.com/rustls/upki/issues/199)). The Go proposal does not _require_ or directly link any `upki` components. We target the C2SP specification for the revocation cache. It may be populated by a system service invoking the rustls `upki` CLI tool, or it may be produced by any other spec compliant process (including a future built-in pure-Go option).

The recent [October 2026 eMudhra incident][emudhra] helps illustrates the need for a solution for out of band PKI data, particularly for non-browser usecases. While browsers and platform trust services quickly distributed blocks for compromised intermediate CAs, other clients, including Go, needed software or trust-store updates. This proposal covers out of band data for leaf certificate revocations as a stepping stone towards also handling intermediate distrust, and other PKI data not captured by todays "big bag of trust anchor PEM" system CA certificate bundles.

## Proposal

Add the following API to `crypto/tls`:

```go
package tls

type Config struct {
    // Existing fields...

    // UPKIRevocation controls checking server certificates against a local
    // UPKI revocation cache.
    //
    // The zero value automatically uses a platform-default cache, if one is
    // available. It rejects certificates reported as revoked, but permits
    // certificates that are not covered by the cache data or contain no
    // embedded SCTs.
    //
    // UPKIRevocation applies only to client-side verification of server
    // certificates.
    UPKIRevocation UPKIRevocationConfig
}

// UPKIRevocationConfig controls local, offline UPKI revocation checking of
// server certificates.
//
// It doesn't configure downloading or updating revocation data.
type UPKIRevocationConfig struct {
    // Disabled disables UPKI revocation checking, including automatic
    // discovery of a platform-default cache.
    Disabled bool

    // Directory is a C2SP upki-revocation cache directory. It contains
    // index.bin directly.
    //
    // If Directory is empty, a platform-default directory is used when one is
    // available.
    Directory string

    // MissingData controls the result when a cache lookup is needed but a
    // selected or explicitly configured cache contains no revocation data.
    MissingData UPKIRevocationPolicy

    // NotCovered controls the result when the revocation data cannot
    // determine a certificate's status. This includes a C2SP "not enrolled"
    // result.
    NotCovered UPKIRevocationPolicy

    // MissingSCTs controls the result when a certificate contains no embedded
    // Signed Certificate Timestamps. It is handled before reading the cache
    // index or filter files.
    MissingSCTs UPKIRevocationPolicy
}

// UPKIRevocationPolicy controls the result of an inconclusive UPKI revocation
// check.
type UPKIRevocationPolicy uint8

const (
    // UPKIRevocationPolicyDefault uses the default for the corresponding
    // UPKIRevocationConfig field.
    UPKIRevocationPolicyDefault UPKIRevocationPolicy = iota

    // UPKIRevocationPolicyAllow allows the connection to proceed when the
    // revocation check is inconclusive for the corresponding reason.
    UPKIRevocationPolicyAllow

    // UPKIRevocationPolicyReject rejects the connection when the revocation
    // check is inconclusive for the corresponding reason.
    UPKIRevocationPolicyReject
)
```

Separate `MissingSCTs` and `NotCovered` policies let a client allow certificates without embedded SCTs, such as private PKI certificates, while requiring conclusive results for certificates with SCTs.

The proposed API surface is deliberately minimal. Standalone X509 certificate revocation checks or APIs for interacting with binary index or clubcard data remain out of scope, and can be offered in third-party packages such as [cpu/go-upki][go-upki].

### Default behaviour

The zero value follows these defaults, informed by the [`rustls-upki`][rustls-policy] defaults for integrating upki with Rustls TLS:

| Condition | Default result | `UPKIRevocationConfig` override |
| --- | --- | --- |
| No platform-default UPKI cache exists | Do not perform UPKI checking | Set `Directory` explicitly and retain the default `MissingData` policy to require a cache for lookups. |
| Certificate contains no embedded SCTs | Allow | `MissingSCTs: UPKIRevocationPolicyReject` |
| A lookup is needed, but a selected or explicit cache has no data | Reject | `MissingData: UPKIRevocationPolicyAllow` |
| Certificate or issuer is not covered | Allow | `NotCovered: UPKIRevocationPolicyReject` |
| Certificate is reported revoked | Always reject | No per-result override; only `Disabled: true`. |
| Certificate is conclusively not revoked | Allow | None |
| Cache or certificate input is malformed | Reject | No per-result override; only `Disabled: true`. |
| Cache is unreadable or internally inconsistent | Reject | No per-result override; only `Disabled: true`. |

`MissingSCTs` is handled before cache reads and malformed SCTs are errors. `MissingData` controls only the outcome when faced with absent data when a lookup is needed. Permission and integrity errors in files needed for a lookup are always fatal while checking is enabled.

Unknown policy values cause a configuration error. Confirmed revocation always rejects the connection unless UPKI checking is disabled entirely, there is no explicit policy to perform revocation checking but ignore a revoked result.

### Cache handling

The cache follows [C2SP `upki-revocation` v1.0.0][c2sp], specifically version 4 clubcard filters and an `upkiidx1` index. Embedded Certificate Transparency Signed Certificate Timestamps (SCTs) select covering filters and a revoked result from any covering filter wins. Go will follow the specified lookup and concurrent-update procedure, opening the current index for each check so published updates take effect without restarting the application.

Any conforming producer, including [`upki fetch`][upki], can provision the cache. Go doesn't download updates, execute or link to Rust code, or read the CLI's `config.toml`. `Directory` names the directory containing `index.bin` and the filter files, typically the CLI cache root's `revocation` child.

`Disabled` takes precedence over `Directory` and prevents cache access. Otherwise, an empty `Directory` selects the first existing user or system cache, in that order, using the current UPKI defaults:

| Platform | User cache | System cache |
| --- | --- | --- |
| XDG/Unix | `$XDG_CACHE_HOME/upki/revocation`, falling back to `$HOME/.cache/upki/revocation` | `/var/cache/upki/revocation` |
| macOS | `$HOME/Library/Caches/dev.rustls.upki/revocation` | `/Library/Caches/dev.rustls.upki/revocation` |
| Windows | `%LOCALAPPDATA%\rustls\upki\cache\revocation` | `%ProgramData%\rustls\upki\cache\revocation` |

With no default cache dir, the added cost is checking candidate paths. If the cache dir is present, lookup reads index metadata, binary-searches the CT log table, and parses only covering filters. The cost of this check depends on the number and size of clubcards, but will not require scanning every filter.

The producer authenticates updates and maintains freshness. SCT coverage describes issuance times, not revocation data age, so a stale cache can miss later revocations for covered certificates.

### TLS verification behaviour

Checking applies only to end-entity server certificates in TLS clients. CRLite covers CT-logged Web PKI certificates, not intermediate certificates or general client authentication.

The check runs after path and hostname verification succeeds and before `VerifyPeerCertificate` and `VerifyConnection`.

Before offering resumption, the client rechecks the certificate using verified chains stored in session state and the current cache and policy. If the check fails, the client attempts a full handshake, allowing the server to present a replacement certificate subject to the same checks.

When `InsecureSkipVerify` disables normal verification, UPKI checking is skipped.

### Future work

Future proposals could add native pure-Go cache fetching and updates. It's also possible to experiment with checks against an online mirror using HTTP range lookups, with separate privacy and availability considerations. It's expected that further UPKI specifications could support intermediate/root distrust, CT policy, intermediate preloading, and Merkle Tree Certificate landmarks. The vision of upki covers much of this, and an integration with Go for leaf revocation checking is a foundation and not an end-state.

[c2sp]: https://c2sp.org/upki-revocation@v1.0.0
[rustls-policy]: https://docs.rs/rustls-upki/latest/rustls_upki/struct.Policy.html
[upki]: https://github.com/rustls/upki
[go-upki]: https://github.com/cpu/go-upki
[go-verify]: https://github.com/golang/go/blob/go1.27.3/src/crypto/x509/verify.go#L566-L592
[go-new-cert-pool]: https://github.com/golang/go/blob/go1.27.3/src/crypto/x509/cert_pool.go#L63-L69
[go-windows]: https://github.com/golang/go/blob/go1.27.3/src/crypto/x509/root_windows.go#L243-L250
[emudhra]: https://emudhra.we-have-a-problem.com/
[canonical]: https://jnsgr.uk/2025/12/addressing-linuxs-missing-pki-infra/

