When a `CompilationTestHelper` test fails, I cannot read what happened on a failing line from one place in the log. With #6117 applied, the failure lists one message per failing line and then `All errors:`, every diagnostic the compilation reported. What the helper expected on a line is in the first part, what it found there is in the second, and I match the two by line number. A line whose diagnostic is missing has no entry in the second part, so its code appears nowhere in the log. The line numbers count lines of the string passed to `addSourceLines`, not lines of the test file, so finding such a line means opening the test and counting inside a text block.

## What the log shows today

#6117 at 6a00e00629 (on master d1652301dd), OpenJDK 21.0.9, Maven 3.9.16. The source has one line of each kind: a marker that is satisfied, a marker whose diagnostic is missing, a marker whose line has a diagnostic with a different text, and a diagnostic with no marker.

```java
package repro;

import com.google.errorprone.CompilationTestHelper;
import com.google.errorprone.bugpatterns.DeadException;
import org.junit.Test;
import org.junit.runner.RunWith;
import org.junit.runners.JUnit4;

@RunWith(JUnit4.class)
public class ReportShapeTest {
  private final CompilationTestHelper helper =
      CompilationTestHelper.newInstance(DeadException.class, getClass());

  @Test
  public void fourKindsOfLine() {
    helper
        .addSourceLines(
            "Test.java",
            """
            class Test {
              void passing() {
                // BUG: Diagnostic contains: Exception created but not thrown
                new RuntimeException();
              }
              void missing() {
                // BUG: Diagnostic contains: Exception created but not thrown
                int a = 1;
              }
              void differentText() {
                // BUG: Diagnostic contains: Exception thrown but not created
                new IllegalStateException();
              }
              void unexpected() {
                new IllegalArgumentException();
              }
            }
            """)
        .doTest();
  }
}
```

`mvn test` prints:

```text
java.lang.AssertionError: 
Did not see an error on line 8 matching Exception created but not thrown.
Did not see an error on line 12 matching Exception thrown but not created.
Saw unexpected error on line 15.
All errors:
/Test.java:4: error: [DeadException] Exception created but not thrown
    new RuntimeException();
    ^
    (see https://errorprone.info/bugpattern/DeadException)
  Did you mean 'throw new RuntimeException();'?

/Test.java:12: error: [DeadException] Exception created but not thrown
    new IllegalStateException();
    ^
    (see https://errorprone.info/bugpattern/DeadException)
  Did you mean 'throw new IllegalStateException();'?

/Test.java:15: error: [DeadException] Exception created but not thrown
    new IllegalArgumentException();
    ^
    (see https://errorprone.info/bugpattern/DeadException)
  Did you mean 'throw new IllegalArgumentException();'?
```

- Line 8, `int a = 1;`, lost its diagnostic, and no part of the message shows that line. A regression test fails this way when the fix it guards is reverted.
- Line 12 has a diagnostic whose text differs from the marker. The first part says only that nothing on line 12 matched; the text that was reported is the second entry under `All errors:`.
- Line 15 appears twice: as a line number in the first part and in full under `All errors:`.
- Line 4 passed, and `All errors:` prints it too.

## What I am asking for

A failure message in which each failing line is one entry that carries what was expected on that line and what was reported there, including the code of the line, so that no entry has to be matched against another part of the message by its number.

Acceptance, on the source above:

- the entry for line 8 contains `int a = 1;`;
- the entry for line 12 contains both `Exception thrown but not created` and the reported `Exception created but not thrown`;
- the diagnostic on line 4 does not appear, since line 4 passed;
- the diagnostic on line 15 appears once.

The first line of each entry stays the text printed today, such as `Did not see an error on line 8 matching Exception created but not thrown.`, so that assertions like `contains("Did not see an error on line 3")` in `CompilationTestHelperTest` keep passing.

Out of scope: which diagnostics satisfy a marker, and the marker syntax. Giving a marker a name that the failure prints is #6144, and it is independent of this request.

## One possible layout

Written by hand for the source above; the layout is yours to choose.

```text
Did not see an error on line 8 matching Exception created but not thrown.
/Test.java:8: no diagnostic was reported on this line
    int a = 1;

Did not see an error on line 12 matching Exception thrown but not created.
/Test.java:12: error: [DeadException] Exception created but not thrown
    new IllegalStateException();
    ^
    (see https://errorprone.info/bugpattern/DeadException)
  Did you mean 'throw new IllegalStateException();'?

Saw unexpected error on line 15.
/Test.java:15: error: [DeadException] Exception created but not thrown
    new IllegalArgumentException();
    ^
    (see https://errorprone.info/bugpattern/DeadException)
  Did you mean 'throw new IllegalArgumentException();'?
```

Each entry is the message printed today, followed by the diagnostics javac reported on that line in javac's own format, or by the line itself where there are none. A diagnostic that belongs to no failing line, such as one without a source position, would go under a closing `Other diagnostics:` block, which is left out when it is empty.

## Related

- #6116 and #6117: the helper reports every failing line instead of the first one. This request builds on #6117.
- #6143 prints the source line under a `Did not see an error` message in the first part of today's message. The layout above would replace it.
- #6144: a name for a marker.

I found no earlier request for this; I searched the tracker, open and closed, for `CompilationTestHelper`, `DiagnosticTestHelper`, `"All errors"`, and `"Did not see an error"`.

